[DOCUMENT]
TITLE: Candidate shape: XState-style machines with guards bound by name

[GRAMMAR]
IMPORT_FROM_FILE: @repo

[MECHANISM]
UID: MECH-DSL-CANDIDATE-XSTATE-MACHINES
TITLE: Candidate shape: XState-style machines with guards bound by name
DEPTH: sketch
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATEMENT: >>>
PROPOSED SHAPE, one of three, from the 2026-08-29 prior-art spike
(SPIKE-DSL-PRIOR-ART). Not chosen. All three show the SAME worked example so
they compare by eye: a DECISION whose STATUS moves from open to accepted only
when a guard holds, a dirty-edge rule over one relation role, one gate CI can
call, and one actor primitive keyed on an ssh identity.

WHAT IT BORROWS. XState statecharts: a machine per node type with states, events and NAMED guards; guard bodies are bound by name outside the machine (XState setup()/provide()), so a higher layer swaps a body without touching the machine.

THE SHAPE.

  { c, lib, ... }: {
    canon.machines.DECISION = {
      stateField = "STATUS";  initial = "open";
      states.open.on.accept = { target = "accepted"; guard = "governorsSettled"; };
      states.accepted.type = "final";                       # no transition may leave it
    };
    canon.guards.governorsSettled = c.and [                 # bodies bound by NAME
      (c.eq (c.var "actor.class") "human")
      (c.every "Governed_By"
        (c.inList (c.var "target.DEPTH") [ "interface-settled" "implemented" "verified" ]))
    ];
    # a higher layer swaps one body, machine untouched (XState machine.provide):
    # canon.guards.governorsSettled = lib.mkForce (c.eq (c.var "actor.class") "human");
    canon.relations.Governed_By.ripple = { onFields = [ "STATEMENT" ]; clearedBy = [ "human" ]; };
    canon.actors = {
      extractor = "ssh-signing-key";
      classes.human = [ "SHA256:AbCd..." ];
      default = "agent";
    };
    canon.gates.merge.require = [ "no-stale-edges" "lifecycle-consistent" ];
    # `scribe transition DECISION-7 accept` fires the event; CI runs `scribe check --gate merge`
  }

HOW IT DECOMPOSES ONTO THE HOOK LAYER (MECH-HOOK-MIDDLEWARE-INTERFACE).
states.<s>.on.<event> -> PRE hook on scribe.change.set for STATUS with to = target; the guard NAME is resolved at Nix eval (assertion: declared) and its body inlined into the hook settings.
type = "final" -> PRE hook denying any set of STATUS away from that value.
ripple, actors, gates -> exactly as in the Jira-style candidate.
<<<
NOTES: >>>
STRENGTHS.
- Named guards give the cleanest overshadow: mkForce one body, every transition that names it follows; XState calls this provide().
- The machine is pure structure, so Nix can render a state diagram and check reachability from the same eval.
- Event names double as a scribe verb (`scribe transition <uid> <event>`), so a raw set of STATUS can be refused as 'not a transition'.

WEAKNESSES.
- Needs one more scribe verb (transition) or a rule that set-of-STATUS is the event; either is a new L2 surface.
- Two files to read to understand one transition (machine + guards); a novice sees an indirection with no payoff on a small canon.
- XState's guard sees only its own machine; here every guard body needs the one-hop neighbour data, so the resemblance is to the config half only.

Recorded from the spike's synthesis without endorsement; the operator weighs the three by reading the shapes.
<<<
